## Registered frontmatter enums — MIGRATED FROM THE YAML PREDECESSOR

> These three blocks were live in the yaml and drive V5 (`enum_value_unknown`). Carrying them over is
> a straight transcription: the declared value sets are IDENTICAL to the yaml's, so V5 asks exactly
> the question it was already asking, against exactly the same vocabulary. [GUIDANCE-SCOPE] below
> previously listed the enums as deliberately-undeclared; that stance was correct while the yaml was
> still live and enforcing them elsewhere, and is superseded now that this document is the only
> profile — leaving them out would have SILENTLY relaxed a rule, which is the failure class this
> whole consolidation exists to close, not a conservative default.

### [ENUM-CONFIDENTIALITY] Frontmatter `confidentiality`

- enum-key: confidentiality
- values:
  - public
  - nda-shareable
  - internal-only
- definition: The FactLattice core confidentiality values, declared here because the yaml declared them; a domain that declares an enum EMPTY and a domain that declares none are different rules, so silence is not a safe transcription of a present block

### [ENUM-ARTIFACT-READINESS] Frontmatter `artifact-readiness`

- enum-key: artifact_readiness
- values:
  - educational
  - system-design
  - code-generation
- extended-values:
  - design-review
  - concept-capture
- definition: core = FactLattice SPEC §1.5; extended = the ComposeIO additions for in-flight design artifacts (`design-review` for a spec drafted for architect review, `concept-capture` for an early pre-design capture). The `{core, extended}` split is an authoring convenience — `Profile.enum_values` unions them

### [ENUM-SCENARIO-PREFIXES] Scenario prefixes

- enum-key: scenario_prefixes
- values:
  - SCEN-
- definition: Registered for future use (T-SC06); no scenario nodes exist in the corpus yet. Carried verbatim from the yaml. ⚠ NOTHING in the engine reads `scenario_prefixes` — it is a declaration with no reader, preserved rather than dropped so its retirement is a decision someone makes rather than a side effect of a file deletion

---

## Registered node-family schemas — MIGRATED FROM THE YAML PREDECESSOR

### [NODEFAMILY-DECISION] Decision / provenance nodes

- section: decision_nodes
- families: CXC-ICD-, DD-
- required:
  - decision
  - rationale
  - status
- recommended:
  - alternatives
  - invalidating_assumption
- definition: A node in these families records a decision and MUST carry what was decided, why, and its status (V3); it SHOULD carry the alternatives weighed and the assumption whose falsification would reopen it (V4). Migrated verbatim from the yaml's `decision_nodes` block
- migration-note: ⚠ The yaml also carried a `field_aliases` map (`alternatives` ← `alternatives-considered`; `invalidating_assumption` ← `invalidating-assumption`). The `.lts` profile encoding has NO field-alias construct and NOTHING in the engine ever read `field_aliases` — it was inert in the yaml too. It is recorded here in prose so the intent survives the migration and can be built deliberately, rather than disappearing with the file that held it

---

## Registered decision lifecycle — MIGRATED FROM THE YAML PREDECESSOR

### [LIFECYCLE-DECISION] The decision pipeline

- section: decision_lifecycle
- pipeline:
  - raised
  - decided
  - spec-authored
  - built
  - committed
  - deployed
- off-pipeline:
  - superseded
  - deferred
  - withdrawn
- decision-prefixes: DEC
- session-key-property: decided-in-session
- definition: The ordered pipeline (index = rank) plus the terminal states that are legitimately OFF it. V6 validates `lifecycle-position` against the union; the SPLIT is what answers "decisions made but never implemented". Migrated verbatim from the yaml's `decision_lifecycle`
- migration-note: ⚠ The yaml also carried `evidence_edge: lifecycle.ADVANCED_TO`. The `.lts` LIFECYCLE encoding has no `evidence-edge` key and NOTHING in the engine read it — inert in the yaml as well. Recorded here so the intended evidence edge is not lost with the file
- caveat: See [GUIDANCE-LIFECYCLE-UNDECLARED] and [GUIDANCE-LIFECYCLE-COMMENTS] — this pipeline is a DECISION-family vocabulary, and the deliverable and strategy families use different value sets against the same property name. Declaring it here reproduces exactly the rule the yaml already enforced and no more; widening it to honestly order all three families is a separate, still-open ratification

---

## Registered relationship verbs

> These are BARE verbs, matching the ComposeIO `[A] VERB [B]` triple idiom ([CONV-RELVERB]).
> Registration is what makes them RECOGNISED: an unregistered bare verb standing between a source id
> and a bracketed target is preserved but flagged `unregistered_verb` (SPEC §2.4 preserve-and-flag),
> so today 299 of the corpus's 804 authored triples carry that flag. Core verbs need no entry here
> and have none — DEPENDS_ON, RELATED_TO, IMPLEMENTS, CONTAINS, SUPERSEDES, PART_OF, BEFORE,
> CAUSES, ENABLES, PRECONDITION_FOR, PREVENTS and CONTRAST_WITH are all core and account for the
> other 505 triples.

### [EDGETYPE-USES] A consumes or applies B

- edge-type: USES
- tier: domain
- definition: A consumes, applies, or is built on the contract B provides
- evidence: 98 authored triples across 17 files

### [EDGETYPE-REALIZES] A makes design B real

- edge-type: REALIZES
- tier: domain
- definition: A is the concrete implementation that makes design or intent B real
- evidence: 59 authored triples across 21 files

### [EDGETYPE-UPHOLDS] A preserves invariant B

- edge-type: UPHOLDS
- tier: domain
- definition: A preserves or enforces the invariant, principle or constraint B
- evidence: 45 authored triples across 16 files

### [EDGETYPE-DOCUMENTS] A is the provenance record for B

- edge-type: DOCUMENTS
- tier: domain
- definition: A is a decision-provenance or rationale record explaining B; the DD- family's characteristic edge
- evidence: 39 authored triples across 9 files
- property-key: documents
- layer: semantic
- property-key-status: ⛔ DECLARED BUT INERT — the engine does not yet READ this. Registered 2026-08-15 under [DEC-PROFILES-MAY-REGISTER-THEIR-OWN-EDGE-KEYS] because `documents:` is already authored 29 times as a PURE single-id reference across 8 files (55-console-launches-the-intelligence 8, 56-terminal-widget 8, 57-substrate-agnostic-subscription-filtering 7, 59-lts-writer 2, 58-document-root-and-element-inflation 2, 61-config-over-bus 1, 54-composeux-ordered-container 2) and mints ZERO edges — the identical shape to the `relates-to` defect, one key over. MEASURED THE SAME DAY: `factlattice.profile.load_profile` projects this correctly (`property_edges == {'documents': ('DOCUMENTS','semantic',False)}`) but NO PRODUCTION CALLER passes `property_edges=` to `parse_file`/`parse_text` — `index_builder.build_index` forwards `profile_layers` and `domain_verbs` only, and `corpus._reparse_dir` forwards `domain_verbs` only. So the parser hook (parser.py:1139) is dead code on every live path, and `lts_preview` with this profile ACTIVE and AVAILABLE mints nothing from `documents:` — verified, not assumed. The declaration is left standing because it is CORRECT and becomes live the moment the caller is wired; until then the 29 relationships stay invisible and DOCUMENTS must be authored as a `## Relationships` triple, which does mint. Engine fix belongs to 740Dave/factlattice (PUBLIC, David's word alone) on branch fix/property-edge-minting-and-profile-registrable-keys.

### [EDGETYPE-EXEMPLIFIES] A is a concrete instance of principle B

- edge-type: EXEMPLIFIES
- tier: domain
- definition: A is a concrete instance of the principle or pattern B. Registered BARE deliberately — `exemplifies_coverage` matches an edge whose type IS the verb or whose namespaced suffix is, and the bare form is what the corpus already authors
- evidence: 34 authored triples across 11 files

### [EDGETYPE-APPLIES-AT-PHASE] A is the move for phase B

- edge-type: APPLIES_AT_PHASE
- tier: domain
- definition: Strategy A is the right working move at context-lifecycle phase B; the reverse traversal is the boot-read query [SBR-BOOT-STRATEGIES]
- evidence: 10 authored triples in strategies.lts, whose own Relationships note flags this verb as awaiting exactly this registration

### [EDGETYPE-EXTENDS] A extends contract B

- edge-type: EXTENDS
- tier: domain
- definition: A extends B's contract rather than forking it — the narrow-waist idiom expressed as an edge
- evidence: 6 authored triples across 6 files

### [EDGETYPE-BUILDS-ON] A builds on B

- edge-type: BUILDS_ON
- tier: domain
- definition: A is layered on B. Singleton usage; a candidate to normalize onto EXTENDS
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 28-composeux-headless-monitor.lts

### [EDGETYPE-CLOSES] A closes open item B

- edge-type: CLOSES
- tier: domain
- definition: A closes the open question or gap B. Singleton usage
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 22-composeux-expression-dsl.lts

### [EDGETYPE-GENERALIZES] A generalizes B

- edge-type: GENERALIZES
- tier: domain
- definition: A is the general form of the specific case B. Singleton usage
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 31-composeux-board-bringup.lts

### [EDGETYPE-MIRRORS] A mirrors B

- edge-type: MIRRORS
- tier: domain
- definition: A deliberately reproduces B's semantics on another tier. Singleton usage; a candidate to normalize onto core SIMILAR_TO
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 22-composeux-expression-dsl.lts

### [EDGETYPE-MITIGATES] A mitigates risk B

- edge-type: MITIGATES
- tier: domain
- definition: A reduces the impact of hypothesis or risk B. Singleton usage; causal in meaning, a candidate to normalize onto core PREVENTS
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 56-terminal-widget.lts

### [EDGETYPE-PORTS] A ports B to another tier

- edge-type: PORTS
- tier: domain
- definition: A carries B's behaviour onto a different substrate. Singleton usage
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 29-composeux-embedded-reactive.lts

### [EDGETYPE-RESERVES] A reserves capability B

- edge-type: RESERVES
- tier: domain
- definition: A holds B in reserve for a later slice. Singleton usage
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 24-composer-flow-headless-authoring.lts

### [EDGETYPE-REUSES] A reuses B

- edge-type: REUSES
- tier: domain
- definition: A reuses B by reference rather than forking it. Singleton usage; a candidate to normalize onto USES
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 23-composeux-widget-primitives.lts

### [EDGETYPE-GROUNDS] A is the physical evidence B was validated against

- edge-type: GROUNDS
- tier: domain
- definition: A is hardware or measured reality that B's design was checked against — the evidence direction, distinct from REALIZES (which runs design -> implementation) and from DOCUMENTS (which is provenance for a decision). Registered because no core or domain verb carries "this design was tested against this physical thing"
- evidence: 1 authored triple in 64-pcb-board-landscape.lts; authored since 2026-07-18 in the source document and carrying `unregistered_verb` the whole time
- ratification: UNRATIFIED 2026-08-13

### [EDGETYPE-STRESSES] A is a case B's model cannot express

- edge-type: STRESSES
- tier: domain
- definition: A is a concrete case that B's model fails to cover or can only express badly — the exact inverse of UPHOLDS, and the verb a coverage gap needs. A corpus that can only say UPHOLDS can record agreement but not the counter-example, which is the more valuable of the two
- evidence: 8 authored triples in 64-pcb-board-landscape.lts, five of them from the per-stress nodes onto the specific spec-32 node each one breaks
- ratification: UNRATIFIED 2026-08-13

---

## Linter guidance

### [GUIDANCE-SCOPE] What this profile declares — SUPERSEDED by the yaml retirement

- note: SUPERSEDED 2026-08-11. The original stance was that this profile registers vocabulary only — namespaces, prefixes, document tags, relationship verbs — and leaves `decision_nodes`, enums and `decision_lifecycle` undeclared so V3/V4/V5/V6 stay silent pending ratification. That was right while `99-composeio-profile.yaml` was still live and still enforcing those four blocks over `corpora/platform-specs`. It stopped being right the moment this document became the ONLY profile: an undeclared block is then not a deferred rule, it is a REPEALED one, and repealing a rule as a side effect of deleting a file is the silent-loss class this consolidation exists to close. All four blocks are now migrated verbatim from the yaml, so the rules in force are exactly the rules that were already in force — no more, no less. `mutation_nodes` remains undeclared because the yaml never declared it either.

### [GUIDANCE-YAML-RETIRED] The yaml predecessor is retired, and what that cost

- note: `corpora/platform-specs/99-composeio-profile.yaml` was DELETED on 2026-08-11 after a full two-way set difference against this document. Migrated in: 18 first-segment prefixes (of which DASH- and REN- were carrying 71 live findings), the AMEND document tag, three enums (confidentiality, artifact_readiness, scenario_prefixes), the decision_nodes schema, and the decision_lifecycle pipeline. Deliberately NOT migrated, with reasons: the SPEC and SYSTEM document tags (SPEC §1.7 core, unioned in by V2 already); the DEPENDS_ON and RELATED_TO relationship verbs (core, in `CORE_EDGE_LAYER`, and registering a core verb as a domain verb is how a domain starts redefining the format); 21 sub-family prefixes such as BP-EXT- and CXC-HALD- that V1's first-segment match could never fire on, folded into their first-segment nodes' definitions as documentation. Carried as prose because the encoding has no construct for them and nothing read them: `decision_nodes.field_aliases`, `decision_lifecycle.evidence_edge`, and `linter_guidance.footer` / `linter_guidance.references` (version_required, status_required, qualified_registered_doctype_ok — all three grep to zero readers in the engine).

### [GUIDANCE-FOOTER-AND-XREF] Footer and cross-reference policy, carried from the yaml

- note: The yaml's `linter_guidance.footer` and `linter_guidance.references` blocks encoded three policies no engine code has ever read, preserved here as notes so they survive the file. [CONV-FOOTER]: a missing **Version** or **Status** footer is a WARNING, never an error (`version_required: false`, `status_required: false`). [CONV-XREF]: a qualified `[SPEC:ID]` / `[AMEND:ID]` cross-document reference with a registered doctype is VALID even when it resolves nowhere in the loaded corpus. [CONV-XREF-NODE]: a bare cross-document node reference carrying a registered prefix is a warning, not an error, when unresolvable.

### [GUIDANCE-PREFIX-FIRST-SEGMENT] Register the first segment, never the sub-family

- note: V1 matches a node's `type_prefix` — the id text before the FIRST hyphen — with trailing hyphens normalized off both sides. A registration of `CXC-IC-` or `BP-EXT-` can therefore NEVER match any node, which is why the yaml predecessor could declare 38 prefixes and validate none of them. Sub-families are documentation; only the first segment is a registration.

### [GUIDANCE-BARE-VERBS] A bare unregistered verb is flagged, not dropped

- note: In the anchored form `[SRC] VERB [TGT]` the parser preserves the edge and flags it `unregistered_verb` (SPEC §2.4). Registering a verb here does not CREATE the edge, it CLEARS the flag and makes the edge recognised. 299 of the corpus's 804 authored triples are in that state before this profile loads.

### [GUIDANCE-LIFECYCLE-UNDECLARED] The lifecycle vocabulary is not one vocabulary

- note: `lifecycle-position` appears 363 times across three DIFFERENT node families with three different value sets — decisions (raised, decided, settled, corrected, observed, proposed, parked, spec-authored, built, implemented, committed, superseded), deliverables (accepted, submitted, reviewed, superseded) and strategies (seeded), plus `fixed` on a defect. A single `decision_lifecycle` pipeline cannot honestly order them, and `made_but_not_implemented` is only meaningful if the ordering is right. Declaring it is David's ratify, not an authoring call.

### [GUIDANCE-LIFECYCLE-COMMENTS] Trailing comments live INSIDE the value

- note: Roughly 25 `lifecycle-position` lines in decision-graph-composeux-architect.lts carry a trailing `# ...` comment, and `.lts` defines no comment form — so the parsed value is the whole string, e.g. `committed   # David glass-confirmed`. Those will not match ANY declared stage whatever the profile says. The fix is to move the comment to its own property, not to widen the enum.

### [GUIDANCE-UNTAGGED-H1] Five documents mint no document node

- note: 40-decisions-tab.lts, 41-intelligence-host-spawner.lts, 42-idle-tracking.lts, insights_and_warts.lts and design-excellence-composeplatform.lts open with an h1 carrying NO `[DOCTYPE:ID]`, so they mint no document node and no `document_tag`. V2 cannot flag them because there is nothing to flag; they are invisible to any query addressing a document by tag.

### [GUIDANCE-DISCOVERY-IS-PER-ROOT] This profile is REGISTERED, not merely discovered

- note: RESOLVED 2026-08-11. `discover_profile` globs `*-profile.lts` in the corpus root NON-recursively, so siting a profile in a directory only ever binds it to THAT directory — which is why this document, sitting in `corpora/intelligence-console`, was dark for `corpora/platform-specs` (which discovered the yaml) and dark again for any lint over a file LIST (which derived no root at all). The fix is registration, not relocation: every composeio corpus now names this file in the `profile` key of its `FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_CONFIG` entry, and `factlattice.profile.resolve_profile_ex` consults the registry BEFORE auto-discovery. Every lint result now reports `profile.resolved_from`, so `corpus-config` versus `directory-auto-discovery` versus `none` is a fact a reader can see rather than infer.

---

**Version**: 2.0 — 2026-08-11. v1.0 was derived by scanning `corpora/**/*.lts` (1155 heading nodes, 804 authored relationship triples, 64 document roots). v2.0 SUPERSEDES and retires `corpora/platform-specs/99-composeio-profile.yaml`, absorbing its full declared vocabulary after a two-way set difference — see [GUIDANCE-YAML-RETIRED] for what was migrated, what was deliberately left out as core, and what was carried as prose because the encoding has no construct for it.
**Status**: Draft — the `.lts` profile encoding itself remains offered-for-ratification per `factlattice/profile.py`. The four blocks migrated from the yaml ([ENUM-*], [NODEFAMILY-DECISION], [LIFECYCLE-DECISION]) are NOT new rules awaiting ratify: they reproduce rules the yaml was already enforcing. Widening the lifecycle vocabulary to honestly cover the deliverable and strategy families ([GUIDANCE-LIFECYCLE-UNDECLARED]) is still open and still David's call.
